{{smaller block|<poem>Thou ''woldist'' not oure loore leere.”</poem>
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 76.}}
That day that a man ''would'' have another’s landes or his goodes, that day he ''would'' have his life also if he could.
{{right|''Darrell Papers'', 1583 (H. Hall, Society in Elizabethan Age, [App. ii.).}}
And ye ''will'' not come to me, that ye might have life.
{{right|John v. 40.}}
Oh, sir, the multitude, that seldom know any thing but their own opinions, speak that they ''would'' have.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Philaster, i. 1.}}
''Will'' you permit the orphan — nephew to whom you have been a father — to offer you a trifle [a ring]?
{{right|''Scott'', Antiquary, xxx.}}}}
'''2.''' To be (am, is, are, etc.) determined (to do, etc.): said when one insists on or persists in being or doing something; hence, must, as a matter of will or pertinacity; do (emphatic auxiliary) from choice, wilfulness, determination, or persistence.
{{smaller block|<poem>Alas, the general might have pardon’d follies!
Soldiers ''will'' talk sometimes.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Valentinian, iv. 1.}}
<poem>Fate’s such a shrewish thing,
She ''will'' be mistris.</poem>
{{right|''Chapman'', Iliad, vi. 498.}}
Some, not contented to haue them [Saxons] a people of German race, ''wil'' needs bring them from elsewhere.
{{right|''Verstegan'', Rest. of Decayed Intelligence (ed. 1628), p. 25.}}
There stand, if thou ''wilt'' stand.
{{right|''Milton'', P. R., iv. 551.}}
If you ''will'' fling yourself under the wheels, Juggernaut will go over you, depend upon it.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Book of Snobs, iii.}}
Cholera, scurvy, and fever, the wound that ''would'' not be heal’d.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Defence of Lucknow.}}}}

